by guillaumedelre at 2026-07-17T15:08:53Z

Adding context I missed in the initial description: this is a **follow-up of #63847**, which introduced the `if (null === $this->callStack) return;` early return in `postProcess()`. That guard correctly stops the method from touching stale internal state after a mid-dispatch `reset()`, but it also skips the `unset($this->wrappedListeners[$eventName])` right below it — leaving every `WrappedListener` created in `preProcess()` orphan in the `wrappedListeners[$eventName][]` array.

Symptom in the real-world use case: a Messenger worker running in debug mode wires `services_resetter->reset()` onto `WorkerRunningEvent` to bound `TraceableAdapter::$calls` accumulation between messages. That reset propagates to `TraceableEventDispatcher::reset()` mid-dispatch on every idle tick. `wrappedListeners['Symfony\Component\Messenger\Event\WorkerRunningEvent']` then grows by N entries per tick (N = listener count), ~700-900 KB per 1000 dispatches.

An alternative to the one-liner in `reset()` would be to move the `unset(...)` before the early return in `postProcess()`, but that would also skip the listener unwrapping logic which #63847 explicitly guards against. Clearing on the next `reset()` is safer — that is when the caller knows the whole tracer state should be dropped anyway.
